QB.push(
{id:1401,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Claude's tokenizer splits text into sub-word units. Roughly how do the newer and older Claude tokenizers compare on English prose?",
o:["A. Both average about 4 characters per token; the difference shows up only on non-English text","B. The newer tokenizer averages roughly 2.5 characters per token against about 4 for the older one, so identical text yields roughly 30% more tokens","C. The newer tokenizer averages roughly 8 characters per token, roughly halving the count for identical text","D. Tokenization is identical across Claude models; only the per-token price differs"],a:1,
e:"A tokenizer splits text into **sub-word units** learned from a training corpus, and Claude's newer tokenizer uses a different vocabulary from the older one. On English prose it averages about **2.5 characters per token** against roughly **4** for the older one, so the same document is roughly **30% more tokens**. Windows and billing are counted in tokens, not characters, so that shift is a real capacity and cost change. **Currency note:** this changed with recent models, which is why any hard-coded chars-per-token constant is model-specific — re-baseline with `/v1/messages/count_tokens` after a model change rather than carrying the old constant forward.",
w:"A — the ratio itself changed; it is not confined to non-English text. C — the newer tokenizer produces more tokens per character, not fewer. D — tokenizer vocabularies differ between model generations, which is exactly why counts shift on migration."},

{id:1402,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team migrates a prompt template, unchanged byte for byte, to a newer model. `/v1/messages/count_tokens` now reports about 31,000 input tokens where the previous model reported about 24,000, and long-document requests that used to fit now fail. What is the best explanation?",
o:["A. The newer model has a smaller context window, so the same prompt no longer fits","B. `count_tokens` on the newer model includes `max_tokens` in its total","C. The template must contain invisible characters that the newer model's validator rejects","D. The newer model uses a different tokenizer whose vocabulary yields around 30% more tokens for the same text, so the prompt genuinely is larger measured in tokens"],a:3,
e:"A token count is a property of **(text, tokenizer)**, not of text alone. The newer tokenizer averages roughly 2.5 characters per token against about 4 for the older one, so unchanged text measures materially larger. `/v1/messages/count_tokens` is tokenizer-specific and returns a single `input_tokens` figure covering messages, system and tools, which is why it reports the increase faithfully. Practical consequence: token budgets, chunk sizes and packing limits are per-model constants and must be re-measured on every model migration, before the first overflow reaches production.",
w:"A — the reported input count rose, and that is a tokenizer property; a change in window size would not alter what `count_tokens` returns for the same text. B — the endpoint returns one `input_tokens` figure covering messages, system and tools, does not require `max_tokens`, and does not include it. C — the failures are token-budget overflows rather than validation errors, and invisible characters would not move a count by thousands."},

{id:1403,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An intake service measures two payloads of almost identical byte length: 12 KB of English prose, and 12 KB of base64-encoded attachment identifiers. `count_tokens` reports about 3,000 tokens for the prose and about 9,000 for the base64. Why?",
o:["A. Tokenizers learn their vocabulary from natural-language corpora, so common words compress into single sub-word tokens while high-entropy base64 fragments into many very short tokens","B. Base64 is billed at a penalty rate because it is treated as binary content","C. The tokenizer decodes base64 back to the original bytes before counting","D. Token counts scale with byte length only for ASCII, and base64 is not ASCII"],a:0,
e:"Sub-word merges are learned by frequency, so text that looks like the training distribution compresses well and text that does not falls back to very short pieces. The same effect drives the poor ratios on minified JSON, UUIDs, hashes, source code and CJK script. The design consequence is that a token budget must be measured on **representative payloads**: a ratio calibrated on English prose will understate a request full of identifiers by a factor of two or three, which is how requests that 'should' fit overflow in production.",
w:"B — there is no content-type penalty rate; the count is higher simply because the text tokenizes badly. C — no decoding takes place; the tokenizer segments the literal characters it is given. D — base64 is ASCII, and byte length is not what determines the token count in either payload."},

{id:1404,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A packing planner precomputes token counts for 40,000 static document chunks with `count_tokens`, stores the table, and uses it to decide how many chunks fit per request. Six months later the pipeline is moved to a newer model and requests start overflowing, even though the planner reports every batch as comfortably within budget. What is the flaw?",
o:["A. `count_tokens` returns an approximate figure, so the table drifts as the corpus is re-indexed","B. Stored token counts expire after 30 days and must be recomputed","C. Token counts are tokenizer-specific, so a table computed under one model's tokenizer is invalid for a model with a different tokenizer and has to be recomputed per model","D. The planner should have stored `max_tokens` added to each chunk's count"],a:2,
e:"The tokenizer is part of the model, so a cached count is only valid for the model it was measured on. Key the cache by **(chunk, model)** and recompute on migration; `count_tokens` is free and has its own request-per-minute limit, so a bulk re-measure is cheap. Two further cautions: the endpoint returns an **estimate**, so plan with headroom rather than to the ceiling; and the window must also accommodate generated output including thinking, which is a per-request reservation rather than something to bake into a per-chunk figure.",
w:"A — an estimate would not produce a systematic, one-directional shortfall across every batch, whereas a tokenizer change would. B — counts do not expire; nothing about the stored number is time-limited. D — output budget genuinely matters for window planning, but folding it into each chunk's stored count would not make counts measured under the old tokenizer correct for the new model."},

{id:1405,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does it mean that Claude tokenizes text into \"sub-word units\"?",
o:["A. Each token corresponds to exactly one word, with punctuation attached to the preceding word","B. Each token corresponds to one Unicode character","C. A token is a learned fragment, so a common word may be a single token while a rare word, an identifier, or a CJK string splits into several","D. Tokens are fixed 4-byte units, which is why four characters per token is a reliable rule"],a:2,
e:"Tokens are variable-length fragments drawn from a vocabulary learned over a training corpus. Frequent words become single tokens; unfamiliar strings decompose into shorter pieces. Two consequences follow directly. First, token count is not derivable from character count, because it depends on how well the content matches the learned vocabulary. Second, the mapping changes between tokenizer generations, so the same text measures differently on different models. The only reliable measurement is `/v1/messages/count_tokens` for the model you are actually calling.",
w:"A — word-level tokenization is not what is used; a single long word can span several tokens. B — character-level tokenization would make counts trivially predictable, which they demonstrably are not. D — tokens are variable-length learned fragments, and the four-characters-per-token figure is a rough average for English prose on one tokenizer generation rather than a definition."},

{id:1406,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An assistant on a 200K-window model handles English support documents comfortably. The same product localised to Japanese begins overflowing on documents of the same on-screen length. What is happening?",
o:["A. CJK text tokenizes into more tokens per visible character than English prose, so a document that looks equivalent is a much larger token payload","B. Japanese text must be sent as base64, which inflates the request","C. The context window is smaller when a request contains non-Latin script","D. Non-Latin scripts consume exactly two tokens per character by definition"],a:0,
e:"Tokenizer vocabularies are dominated by frequent sub-words from the training distribution, which skews heavily to Latin-script text. Scripts that are under-represented therefore fragment into more, shorter tokens per visible character. The window did not change; the measured size of the content did. The fix is operational: measure token budgets **per locale** with `count_tokens` on real localised documents, size chunking and retrieval per locale rather than globally, and never derive a budget from an English sample.",
w:"B — no encoding change is required; text is transmitted as UTF-8 JSON in every language. C — the window is a fixed property of the model and does not vary by script. D — there is no fixed per-character rate; the inflation factor varies by script, by content and by tokenizer generation, which is precisely why it must be measured."},

{id:1407,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A retrieval pipeline requires chunks of at most 1,000 tokens. The engineer implements the splitter as \"cut every 4,000 characters\", reasoning that four characters make a token. On the current model, a large fraction of chunks are rejected as oversized. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Reduce the cut to 3,500 characters to leave headroom","B. Strip whitespace and punctuation before cutting, which restores the four-to-one ratio","C. Cut on word boundaries instead of raw character offsets","D. Split on measured token counts — accumulate with `/v1/messages/count_tokens` for the model in use and cut when the running total reaches the budget"],a:3,
e:"A character threshold is a proxy for the wrong quantity. At roughly 2.5 characters per token on the newer tokenizer, 4,000 characters is around 1,600 tokens, so the splitter overshoots by more than half on ordinary prose and worse on identifier-dense text. Measure the quantity you are constrained by. `count_tokens` is free and has its own request-per-minute limit independent of message creation, so batch the measurement for bulk indexing and cache the result keyed by chunk and model.",
w:"A — a smaller constant is still a guess against a ratio that varies by content and tokenizer, and 3,500 characters can still exceed 1,000 tokens on dense text. B — stripping characters alters the text you are indexing and still does not make the ratio predictable. C — word boundaries prevent mid-word cuts but say nothing about the token size of the resulting chunk."},

{id:1408,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A gateway calls `/v1/messages/count_tokens` on every inbound request to pre-check budget before forwarding to `/v1/messages`. Under load the pre-check itself starts returning 429s while message creation is nowhere near its own limits. What applies?",
o:["A. `count_tokens` shares a rate limit pool with message creation, so the pre-check is stealing capacity from it","B. `count_tokens` is free but has its own request-per-minute limit independent of message creation, so a per-request pre-check can exhaust that limit on its own — cache counts for stable prefixes and measure only what varies","C. `count_tokens` is billed per call, so the 429 indicates a spend limit was reached","D. `count_tokens` must be called with `max_tokens`, and omitting it causes throttling"],a:1,
e:"`/v1/messages/count_tokens` is **free** and returns exactly one field, `input_tokens`, covering messages, system and tools — but free is not unlimited: it carries its own **RPM limit, independent of message creation**. A blanket per-request pre-check therefore becomes its own bottleneck, and it does not exercise prompt caching either. The economical pattern is to measure once for static prefixes such as system prompts and tool definitions, cache those counts by model, and call the endpoint only for the volatile portion of each request.",
w:"A — the two endpoints do not share a pool, which is exactly why message creation is unaffected here. C — the endpoint is free, and a spend limit would not surface as a 429 on it. D — it does not require `max_tokens`, and a missing required parameter would be a 400 rather than a 429."},

{id:1409,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A model with a 200K-token context window receives a request whose input measures about 190,000 tokens, with `max_tokens: 16000`. That `max_tokens` value is well inside the model's output ceiling. What should the team expect?",
o:["A. The request is accepted, but generation can only run about 10,000 tokens before the window is exhausted, at which point the response comes back truncated with `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`","B. `max_tokens` is silently clamped to 10,000 and the response ends with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`","C. The request is rejected with 413 `request_too_large`, because input plus `max_tokens` exceeds the window","D. Output is budgeted separately from input, so all 16,000 output tokens are available"],a:0,
e:"The context window bounds the request **plus everything generated in the turn, including thinking tokens** — output is not a separate allowance. On current models the API **accepts** a request whose input plus `max_tokens` exceeds the window and only stops if generation actually reaches the limit, surfacing `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`. Input that alone exceeds the window is a different failure: a 400 `invalid_request_error`. Design consequence: budget as input plus output plus thinking, leave genuine headroom, and branch on that stop reason as a first-class outcome — the enum is extensible, so keep a default branch too.",
w:"B — there is no silent clamping; `max_tokens` is either honoured as an output cap or rejected outright when it exceeds the model ceiling. C — 413 `request_too_large` concerns request size limits, not the window arithmetic, and this request is accepted. D — output shares the window with input and thinking, and treating it as a separate budget is precisely the assumption that produces this truncation."},

{id:1410,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A long-document summariser on a 200K-window model intermittently returns `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`. Logs show input between 185,000 and 199,000 tokens. The team halves `max_tokens` from 8,000 to 4,000 and the failure rate barely moves. Why?",
o:["A. `model_context_window_exceeded` is a transient capacity signal and should be retried rather than tuned","B. `max_tokens` is ignored on models that support this stop reason","C. Overflow is driven by the dominant term, and here that is the input — with 199,000 tokens of input there is almost no window left whatever the output cap is, so the lever is reducing input rather than trimming output","D. The API would have rejected the request up front if the arithmetic mattered, so the cause must lie elsewhere"],a:2,
e:"The window holds everything: system prompt, every message including tool results, images and documents, tool definitions, and everything generated in the turn including thinking (which is a subset of `max_tokens` and billed as output). When input consumes 99% of the window, shaving the output cap cannot create room. Real levers are all input-side: chunk the document and aggregate in code, retrieve fewer and better passages, clear stale tool results, move to a larger-window tier. Principle: when the window binds, attack the largest term — which is almost always input.",
w:"A — it is a deterministic budget outcome, not an overload condition; the same request overflows the same way every time. B — `max_tokens` is honoured; it simply is not the term exhausting the window here. D — current models accept requests whose input plus `max_tokens` exceeds the window and surface the overflow during generation; only input that alone exceeds the window is rejected up front, as a 400."},

{id:1411,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which statement about output tokens and the context window is correct?",
o:["A. The window bounds the request; generated output is metered separately, against `max_tokens` only","B. The window bounds the request plus everything generated in the turn, including thinking tokens; `max_tokens` is an additional cap on generated output, not a separate allowance","C. Thinking tokens are excluded from the window because the raw reasoning is not returned to the client","D. The window bounds only the `messages` array; `system` and `tools` are counted separately"],a:1,
e:"The window is a single budget covering the whole turn. Everything you send counts — system prompt, all messages including tool results, images and documents, and tool definitions — and so does everything the model generates, including thinking tokens, which are a subset of `max_tokens` and billed as output. Cached prefixes still occupy the window; caching changes what you pay for those tokens, not whether they count. `/v1/messages/count_tokens` returns one `input_tokens` figure spanning messages, system and tools for exactly this reason.",
w:"A — output shares the window with input; `max_tokens` narrows the output cap but does not create a second budget. C — thinking tokens occupy the window and are billed as output whether or not the raw reasoning is returned. D — messages, system and tools all occupy the same window, which is why the token-counting endpoint reports them as one figure."},

{id:1412,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A report generator sometimes returns output that stops mid-sentence. One sampled response carries `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`; another carries `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`. What is the correct reading?",
o:["A. They are aliases for the same condition, and either should be handled by retrying","B. Both mean the input was too large, so the prompt should be reduced in both cases","C. Both mean `max_tokens` was too low, so it should be raised in both cases","D. The first means the response hit the per-request output cap you configured, so raising `max_tokens` can help; the second means the model ran out of context window during generation, so raising `max_tokens` cannot help and the input must shrink"],a:3,
e:"Two different budgets produce two different stop reasons with opposite remedies. `max_tokens` is the output cap you set: hitting it means the model had window to spare and your ceiling was the constraint. `model_context_window_exceeded` means the shared window covering input plus generated output including thinking was exhausted, so asking for more output tokens only requests space that does not exist. Branch on the field, never on the response text, and keep a **default branch** — the stop-reason enum is documented as extensible and can grow.",
w:"A — they have different causes and different remedies, and neither is a transient condition that retrying resolves. B — shrinking the prompt does nothing for a response cut off by your own output cap with window still available. C — raising `max_tokens` against an already-exhausted window asks for tokens there is no room for."},

{id:1413,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent loop resends the full accumulated history on every iteration. Iterations 1 to 20 run cleanly; around iteration 40 the calls begin failing on context. Token logs show input growing by 3,000 to 6,000 tokens per iteration, almost all of it tool results. Which change addresses the cause?",
o:["A. Increase `max_tokens` so each turn has room to finish","B. Switch to a streaming request so the response is not held in memory","C. Retry the failing iteration with a fresh conversation each time","D. Bound the history the loop resends — drop or summarise stale tool output — so accumulated input stops growing without limit"],a:3,
e:"The Messages API is **stateless**: there is no server-side conversation, so every iteration pays for the whole transcript again and the transcript is exactly what grows. Unbounded growth is therefore an architectural property of the loop, not a defect in any single call. Every remedy is about what you choose to resend: clear old tool results, summarise completed sub-tasks, keep durable state in your own store and re-inject only what the next step needs. Principle: in a stateless API, context is a budget your loop spends, so something has to decide what to forget.",
w:"A — the failure is that input plus output no longer fits, and requesting more output makes it worse. B — streaming changes how the response is delivered, not how many tokens the request occupies. C — starting fresh discards the state the agent depends on and does nothing to stop the same growth recurring."},

{id:1414,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A request sets `max_tokens` above the model's maximum output length. What happens?",
o:["A. The value is silently clamped to the model ceiling and generation proceeds","B. The request is rejected with a 400 `invalid_request_error`; there is no silent clamping","C. The value is accepted and the excess is charged at a premium rate","D. The response returns with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` and empty content"],a:1,
e:"An out-of-range `max_tokens` is a validation failure, returned as **400 `invalid_request_error`**. The absence of clamping is deliberate: a silently reduced cap would let a misconfigured service ship truncated responses while believing it had asked for more. Contrast this with the window arithmetic, where current models do accept input plus `max_tokens` exceeding the window and surface overflow at generation time as `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`. Principle: configuration errors should fail loudly; runtime budget exhaustion is reported in the response.",
w:"A — no clamping occurs, precisely so that a misconfigured cap cannot hide truncation. C — there is no overflow rate, because the request never runs. D — an empty successful response would conceal a configuration error that the API instead surfaces as a validation failure."},

{id:1415,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A CI suite asserts that a summarisation endpoint returns a byte-identical string for a fixed input. It passes locally and fails roughly one run in eight in CI. The model ID is pinned. What is the right response?",
o:["A. Set `temperature: 0` on the request so the output becomes deterministic","B. Retry the assertion up to three times and pass if any attempt matches","C. Replace exact-match assertions with property and rubric checks — required fields present, length bounds, no forbidden content, semantic match against a reference — because generation is probabilistic and byte-equality is not a property the API offers","D. Pin a model snapshot with a date suffix so the weights cannot change"],a:2,
e:"Generation **samples** from a distribution over next tokens, so identical inputs need not yield identical bytes; pinning a model ID fixes the weights, not the sampled output, and serving infrastructure can change underneath a pinned ID. Test the properties you actually depend on and keep an eval set with a pass threshold rather than a golden file. **Currency note:** `temperature`, `top_p` and `top_k` are deprecated and rejected with a 400 on current models, and even where `temperature: 0` was supported the output was never fully deterministic.",
w:"A — sampling parameters are deprecated and return a 400 on current models, and even at zero they never guaranteed byte-identical output. B — a flaky assertion retried until it passes tests nothing and masks genuine regressions. D — current model IDs are dateless pinned snapshots, appending a date suffix is invalid, and pinning would not make sampling deterministic in any case."},

{id:1416,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team pins a dateless model ID, caches a byte-identical prefix, holds `output_config.effort` constant and changes no prompt text. Over four months, a small share of responses to a fixed regression input come back worded differently. Engineering insists that a pinned ID makes change impossible. What is the accurate account?",
o:["A. A pinned ID fixes the model weights, but the serving infrastructure behind it can change; combined with probabilistic sampling, pinning buys behavioural stability rather than bit-identical output","B. A pinned ID guarantees identical output, so the drift proves the requests were not actually identical","C. Prompt caching returns whatever text was cached, so drift means cache entries were corrupted","D. Dateless IDs are rolling aliases that silently move to the newest snapshot"],a:0,
e:"Two independent sources of variation survive pinning. Sampling means identical inputs need not produce identical bytes at all. Separately, the **serving infrastructure** behind a pinned ID can change even though the weights for that snapshot do not, so long-run drift is expected rather than anomalous. What pinning gives you is that behaviour will not shift because a newer model silently replaced the old one. Build for it: assert properties, not bytes; keep a versioned eval set; and persist request and response pairs when you need a reproducible record.",
w:"B — identical inputs are not a guarantee of identical bytes, since sampling alone breaks that before any infrastructure change. C — the cache stores computed prefix state to reduce input cost, not the generated response, so a cache read still samples fresh output. D — current IDs are pinned snapshots rather than aliases; there are no `-latest` aliases and no date suffix to append."},

{id:1417,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Current Claude model IDs are dateless. What does pinning one actually guarantee?",
o:["A. Byte-identical output for identical requests","B. Fixed model weights for that snapshot, so behaviour is stable — but serving infrastructure can change and sampling is probabilistic, so output is not bit-identical","C. Nothing, because dateless IDs are aliases that roll forward to the latest release","D. That the ID will resolve to a newer model automatically when one ships, preserving compatibility"],a:1,
e:"Each current ID is itself a **pinned snapshot**: there are no `-latest` aliases and you never append a date suffix. What the pin fixes is the weights, and therefore the model's behaviour profile — capability, style, refusal behaviour. What it does not fix is the exact bytes, because sampling is probabilistic and the serving infrastructure behind the ID can change. Design accordingly: pin for stability of behaviour, test with property and eval checks, and log request and response pairs where you need reproducibility.",
w:"A — sampling and serving-side change both break byte-equality; stability of behaviour is the guarantee on offer. C/D — dateless current IDs are pinned snapshots rather than rolling aliases, and appending a date suffix to one is invalid."},

{id:1418,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team verifies prompt caching by sending the same request twice and diffing the response text. The second response differs in wording, while its `usage` shows a large `cache_read_input_tokens` and a small `cache_creation_input_tokens`. They conclude the cache is broken. Are they right?",
o:["A. No — the cache demonstrably worked, as `cache_read_input_tokens` shows; it reuses computed state over the input prefix and has no bearing on the sampled output, which varies run to run","B. Yes — a cache hit must return the previously generated response, so differing text means the entry was invalidated","C. No — but the correct check is that response text matches, and a mismatch means the prefix was not byte-identical","D. Yes — reading from cache while also writing means the entry is thrashing and nothing is being reused"],a:0,
e:"Prompt caching is an **input-side** optimisation: it reuses the computation over a byte-identical prefix, charged at a fraction of base input, and then generation proceeds and samples normally. There is no response cache, so identical text across two calls was never the contract. Verify with the `usage` fields — a large `cache_read_input_tokens` against a small `cache_creation_input_tokens` is exactly the healthy signature. Principle: verify a mechanism by the telemetry it actually emits, not by a proxy it was never designed to affect.",
w:"B — the cache stores prefix computation rather than responses, so there is no stored response to return. C — a byte-identical prefix is what produced the cache read that `usage` already confirms, and output text is not evidence either way. D — a large read with a small write is the healthy pattern; thrashing looks like creation tokens on nearly every call and almost no reads."},

{id:1419,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An auditor requires that the team be able to establish, six months later, exactly what a Claude-backed decision engine told a customer. Which design satisfies that?",
o:["A. Re-run the stored prompt against the same pinned model ID whenever the auditor asks","B. Store the prompt and fix the sampling parameters so the response can be regenerated on demand","C. Store a hash of the prompt plus the model ID, from which the response can be derived","D. Persist the full request, the full response, the model ID, the `request-id` and the `usage` figures at the time of the call, and treat that record as the artefact of record"],a:3,
e:"Because generation is probabilistic and serving infrastructure can change behind a pinned ID, **regeneration is not reproduction** — a re-run is a fresh sample that may legitimately differ from what the customer saw. Auditability therefore comes from logging, not recomputation. Capture the exact request, the exact response, the model ID, the `request-id` header for escalation with support, and the `usage` token counts for cost attribution. Principle: for anything you may have to defend later, log the artefact rather than promising to recreate it.",
w:"A — a re-run is a new sample against possibly changed serving infrastructure, so it can differ from what was shown to the customer. B — sampling parameters are deprecated and rejected on current models, and they never made output byte-reproducible. C — a hash proves the prompt was not tampered with but contains no information from which any response could be derived."},

{id:1420,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A legal research assistant returns a fluent memo with three correctly formatted citations, each carrying a court, a year and a docket number. A paralegal finds that one docket number corresponds to no case. The existing guardrail already validates each citation object against a JSON schema. What closes the gap?",
o:["A. Raise `output_config.effort` so the model reasons harder about its citations","B. Add a system-prompt rule requiring the model to cite only real cases","C. Referential validation — resolve every citation against the authoritative case database and reject or strip any that does not exist, in deterministic code outside the model","D. Enable native structured output with `output_config.format` so the schema is enforced by constrained decoding"],a:2,
e:"Schema validity is a statement about **shape**, not about truth. A well-formed docket number that denotes nothing is schema-valid by construction, so no amount of schema enforcement detects it. The only mechanism that can is a lookup against the system of record: resolve the identifier, confirm the metadata matches, and fail closed when it does not. Principle: **the model proposes, deterministic code authorises** — and validation of a reference means dereferencing it, not inspecting its format.",
w:"A — deeper reasoning may lower the rate but cannot establish that an identifier exists, which requires a lookup. B — prompt instructions are advisory rather than enforcement, and the model cannot consult a database it has no access to. D — constrained decoding makes output schema-valid, which is already the case here; a fabricated docket number is schema-valid."},

{id:1421,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A policy assistant tells an employee the mileage rate is 45p per mile. That figure is exactly what the retrieved handbook page says; the handbook was superseded eight months ago. Is this a hallucination, and what is the remedy?",
o:["A. It is a hallucination; add a rule telling the model not to state rates it cannot verify","B. It is a hallucination; move to a stronger model tier so it reasons more carefully about currency","C. It is not a hallucination — the model faithfully reproduced its source, so the defect is in the corpus, and the fix is retrieval-side: retire superseded documents, index effective dates, filter to current versions","D. It is not a hallucination, and no remedy is needed because the model reported its source accurately"],a:2,
e:"Two failure modes look identical to the user and have opposite remedies. **Hallucination** is generating content the source material does not support, remedied by grounding and output validation. **Faithful reproduction of a wrong source** is a data-quality failure, remedied by curating, versioning and filtering the corpus. The diagnostic is mechanical: check the answer against the retrieved span — if the span supports it, the model did its job and the corpus did not. Principle: grounding can only ever be as correct as what you ground in.",
w:"A — the model invented nothing, so an instruction aimed at invention targets the wrong mechanism and the same stale page is retrieved next time. B — a stronger model reading the same superseded page returns the same superseded figure. D — the answer given to the employee was wrong, so a remedy is plainly required; it simply belongs in the corpus rather than the model."},

{id:1422,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team enables native structured output with `output_config.format` set to a JSON schema and reports that \"extraction is now verified — every field is validated.\" An audit of 500 invoices finds 11 with a supplier VAT number that is well-formed but belongs to a different company. What did the guarantee actually cover?",
o:["A. Nothing — schema enforcement is best-effort and can emit invalid JSON under load","B. It covered the values, so the 11 cases must be upstream OCR errors rather than model output","C. It covered the values only for fields marked `required` in the schema","D. Constrained decoding guarantees the response conforms to the schema — shape, types, required fields, enum membership — and says nothing whatever about whether the values are true"],a:3,
e:"`output_config.format` with a `json_schema` uses **constrained decoding**, so schema conformance is a real guarantee rather than best-effort, and it needs no beta header (`strict: true` is the tool-side equivalent). But conformance is syntax. A syntactically perfect VAT number attached to the wrong company is exactly the class of error a shape constraint cannot see. Truth requires **referential validation** against a system of record: resolve the VAT number, cross-check it against the purchase order, and fail closed on mismatch.",
w:"A — constrained decoding does guarantee schema-valid output, and understating it is as misleading as overstating it. B — a well-formed number belonging to the wrong company is precisely the failure a shape constraint cannot catch, whatever the digits' provenance. C — `required` controls whether a field must be present, not whether its content is correct."},

{id:1423,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An offline eval grades a product-support assistant with an LLM judge scoring each answer 1 to 5 for clarity, tone and coherence. Scores average 4.6. In production the assistant confidently recommends accessory part numbers the catalogue does not contain. What is the flaw in the eval?",
o:["A. It measures fluency, which is a property of the writing, and never checks whether claims are supported by the catalogue — groundedness has to be scored against source data, not against a reader's impression","B. The judge model is too weak; a stronger judge would notice the invented part numbers","C. Averaging the three sub-scores masks a low clarity score on the failing cases","D. LLM judges cannot be used for production evals and should be replaced by human review"],a:0,
e:"**Fluency is not grounding.** Clarity, tone and coherence are all properties of the prose and are perfectly compatible with fabricated content — indeed fluency is what makes fabrication convincing. To measure grounding you must supply the source: score each claim for attribution against the retrieved passages, and add a deterministic existence check for identifiers like part numbers against the catalogue. Principle: an eval measures only what its rubric asks about, so make the failure mode you fear an explicit metric.",
w:"B — a stronger judge scoring the same three dimensions still has no catalogue to check against; the missing input is source data, not judge capability. C — the failing answers are clear, well-toned and coherent, which is the whole point, so no sub-score would be low. D — LLM judges are useful; this one was asked the wrong question, which is a rubric problem rather than a method problem."},

{id:1424,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"In an LLM application, what does it mean for an answer to be \"grounded\"?",
o:["A. That it is written in a confident, well-structured, professional tone","B. That every substantive claim is supported by specific provided or retrieved source content, and that the support can be checked","C. That it was produced by a pipeline with retrieval enabled somewhere in it","D. That it conforms to the output schema the application requires"],a:1,
e:"Grounding is a **checkable** relationship between a claim and a span of source content. That definition matters because it tells you what to build: retrieve the evidence, require the answer to point at the span it relies on, and verify the span supports the claim before the answer is released. Confidence, structure and schema validity are all orthogonal properties that a fabricated answer can satisfy in full. Principle: if a property cannot be verified mechanically, it is not a control.",
w:"A — tone is a property of prose, and entirely unsupported text can be fluent and confident. C — enabling retrieval makes grounding possible but does not establish that the answer used or reflected what was retrieved. D — schema conformance is about shape, and a fabricated value can be perfectly schema-valid."},

{id:1425,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Two incidents land in the same sprint. In the first, an internal assistant describes a `POST /v1/accounts/merge` endpoint that has never existed in the company's API. In the second, it quotes a rate limit accurately from an onboarding guide that was replaced last quarter. Which pair of remedies fits?",
o:["A. The first is unsupported generation — fix with grounding plus post-generation validation of every named endpoint against the API spec; the second is faithful reproduction of a stale source — fix by retiring and version-filtering the corpus","B. Both are hallucinations — add one system-prompt instruction to state only verified facts","C. The first is a corpus problem and the second is a model problem, so move to a stronger tier","D. Both are retrieval failures — increase the number of retrieved passages so more evidence is available"],a:0,
e:"Diagnose by asking whether the retrieved source supports the claim. If nothing supports it, the model generated unsupported content: remedy with grounding and validation, here a lookup of every endpoint name against the published spec. If a source does support it but the source is wrong, the model behaved correctly and the corpus is at fault: remedy with retirement, effective-dating and version filters. The same visible symptom, two mechanisms, two owners. Principle: fix the layer where the error was actually introduced.",
w:"B — one instruction cannot address two different mechanisms, and prompt instructions enforce nothing in either case. C — the diagnosis is reversed: the invented endpoint is unsupported generation, and the stale rate limit came from the corpus. D — retrieving more passages does not prevent invention and actively raises the chance of surfacing the stale guide again."},

{id:1426,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A grounded question-answering service returns answers with a citation to a specific retrieved passage. Spot checks show that in about 7% of cases the cited passage is real and topically relevant but does not contain the claim. Which check catches this class of error?",
o:["A. Verify that the citation identifier appears in the retrieved set","B. Verify claim-level attribution — require the answer to quote or point at the specific span it relies on, then confirm that span in the source supports the claim","C. Verify that the response validates against the citation schema","D. Verify that the retriever's similarity score for the cited passage exceeds a threshold"],a:1,
e:"There are three separable properties of a citation: well-formed, resolvable, and **supporting**. Only the third is grounding, and only span-level attribution tests it. Practically: have the model emit the quoted span alongside each claim, then check in code that the span occurs in the cited source and that the claim is entailed by it, failing closed when it is not. Principle: verification must operate at the granularity of the claim, because a document-level citation is compatible with an unsupported sentence.",
w:"A — the citation identifier is valid here, since the passage exists and was retrieved, which is why identifier checks pass. C — schema validation confirms the citation is well-formed, not that it supports the sentence attached to it. D — a high similarity score means the passage is topically close, which is exactly the condition under which this error arises."},

{id:1427,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To reduce fabricated details, a team adds to the system prompt: \"Do not hallucinate. State only facts you are certain of. If unsure, say you do not know.\" The measured fabrication rate falls from 9% to 8.2%. What is the correct conclusion?",
o:["A. The instruction is right but must be repeated at the end of the prompt to survive attention dilution","B. The model is ignoring the instruction, which indicates a defect in instruction following","C. Instructions can shift the distribution slightly but cannot make the model a verifier of its own claims; measurable reductions come from supplying the evidence and validating the output in code","D. Fabrication is irreducible below roughly 8%, so the team should accept the residual"],a:2,
e:"The model has no internal oracle for truth and no calibrated signal for its own uncertainty, so \"be certain\" is advice rather than a mechanism. What moves the number: retrieve the facts into context so there is something to be grounded in; require span-level attribution; validate identifiers and figures against a system of record; and make **abstention** a code path the system can take when no supporting evidence is found. Principle: the model is not a correctness boundary — prompting shifts the rate, code enforces the requirement.",
w:"A — placement genuinely helps instruction-adherence problems, but no placement converts an advisory instruction into a verification step. B — a small shift is the expected result of a prompt-level nudge, not evidence of a following defect. D — the rate is a property of the architecture rather than a floor, and grounding plus validation moves it substantially."},

{id:1428,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"Which design most directly enables a grounded assistant to answer \"I don't know\" rather than guess?",
o:["A. A system-prompt instruction to admit uncertainty whenever the model is not confident","B. Asking the model to attach a self-reported confidence score and suppressing answers below a threshold","C. Raising `output_config.effort` so the model reasons for longer before answering","D. A deterministic gate in application code: if retrieval returns no passage that supports the question, return the abstention response without asking the model to answer at all"],a:3,
e:"Abstention has to be a decision the **system** makes, because self-assessed uncertainty is unreliable and prompt instructions are advisory. Structure it as control flow: retrieve, apply a relevance and support test, and only then let the model answer over the evidence that was found — otherwise short-circuit to a fixed response. A self-reported confidence number is generated text like any other and is not calibrated. Principle: the model proposes, deterministic code authorises — including the decision not to answer.",
w:"A — the instruction is advisory and presupposes a reliable internal uncertainty signal that does not exist. B — a generated confidence figure is itself model output and is not calibrated, so thresholding it filters unpredictably. C — more reasoning over the same absent evidence yields a more elaborate guess, not an abstention."},

{id:1429,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A market-research assistant returns answers with source URLs. A QA script confirms that every URL returns HTTP 200. A reviewer nonetheless finds answers whose cited page never mentions the claimed figure. What is missing?",
o:["A. A stricter URL schema so malformed links are rejected","B. A retry that re-asks the model whenever a URL returns 404","C. A system-prompt rule requiring the model to cite only pages it has read","D. Content-level verification — fetch the page, locate the span supporting the specific claim, and reject the answer when no span supports it"],a:3,
e:"Reachability is not support. A URL check establishes that a resource exists; it says nothing about whether that resource contains the claim attached to it, which is the only property that matters for grounding. Close the loop by fetching the cited content and testing entailment against the specific claim, then failing closed. Note the trust boundary too: fetched pages are untrusted content and belong in a `user` message inside tags labelled as data, never in the system prompt.",
w:"A — the URLs are already well-formed and resolvable, so shape validation is not the failing check. B — nothing is returning 404, so the retry never fires; a live URL pointing at an unrelated page is the actual failure. C — an instruction cannot make the model verify the contents of a page it never fetched."},

{id:1430,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A contract analyser runs on a 1M-token model. Given a single 6,000-token contract, it extracts the indemnity cap correctly every time. Given 60 contracts totalling 400,000 tokens in one request, it returns the wrong cap for clauses in the middle of the payload while handling the first and last contracts well. What is happening?",
o:["A. The request exceeded the window, so middle content was silently dropped","B. Token counts are estimates, so the middle of the payload was mis-segmented","C. Attention is a finite resource spread across the whole context — fitting inside the window does not mean the model reasons equally well over all of it, and position within a very long payload measurably affects recall of a specific detail","D. Prompt caching served a stale prefix covering the middle contracts"],a:2,
e:"Two properties are separate: **does it fit**, and **can the model reason well over what fits**. Only the first is answered by the window size and `count_tokens`. As token count grows, accuracy and recall degrade — documented as context rot — and detail-level recall varies with position and volume. The practical fix is to reduce what is in context per question: retrieve the relevant clauses, or run one contract per request and aggregate deterministically in code. Principle: more context is not better context.",
w:"A — nothing is silently dropped; overflow surfaces as an explicit `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`, and 400,000 tokens sits well inside a 1M window. B — an estimate of size neither relocates nor corrupts content. D — a cache read reuses computation over a byte-identical prefix and cannot substitute different contract text."},

{id:1431,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A support assistant is instructed in the top-level `system` parameter to answer in at most three sentences and always end with a ticket reference. Compliance is near-perfect for the first ten turns and degrades noticeably by turn fifty, with no prompt change. What is the best explanation and fix?",
o:["A. The system prompt is evicted from context once the conversation exceeds a threshold, so it must be re-sent as a user message","B. Instruction adherence decays as accumulated conversation grows and dominates the context; restate the binding constraints close to the end of the request, and enforce the hard ones in code after generation","C. The model has learned the user's preferred style from the conversation, which overrides the system prompt by design","D. `system` instructions apply only to the first assistant turn and must be repeated in every user message to stay active"],a:1,
e:"Nothing evicts the system prompt — it is resent and counted on every stateless request — but as thousands of tokens of dialogue accumulate, its relative influence falls while later content competes for attention. Effective measures: keep the durable instruction set short and stable at the front so it stays cacheable, restate the two or three non-negotiable constraints late in the request where recency helps, bound history growth, and validate hard requirements such as length and required suffix deterministically after the call. Principle: prompting reduces the rate; code enforces the requirement.",
w:"A — nothing evicts the system prompt; it is transmitted and counted on every request. C — there is no mechanism by which conversational drift is designed to override operator instructions; the effect is dilution, not precedence. D — a top-level `system` parameter applies to the whole request every time it is sent, so per-message repetition is not needed for it to be active."},

{id:1432,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To improve an internal helpdesk assistant, a team stops retrieving and instead pastes the entire 300,000-token company wiki into every request. Answer accuracy falls from 78% to 64% and cost per query rises sharply. What is the correct diagnosis?",
o:["A. More context is not better context — the relevant passage is now buried in vast irrelevant material that competes for attention, and every query pays for all of it","B. The wiki exceeded the window, so the model saw only a truncated prefix","C. The wiki should have been placed in the system prompt rather than in a user message","D. The model needs a higher effort setting to process the larger context"],a:0,
e:"Retrieval is a **precision** mechanism, and replacing it with bulk inclusion trades precision for volume. Accuracy falls because signal-to-noise in context falls, and cost rises because the whole payload is billed on every request in a stateless API. Restore retrieval, tune it for precision rather than recall, and use caching for genuinely static prefixes with volatile content placed after the last breakpoint. Principle: curate what is in context — how much space is available is the weaker constraint.",
w:"B — 300,000 tokens fits a large-window model, and overflow would surface explicitly rather than as a quiet accuracy decline. C — bulk reference material in the system prompt elevates its authority without improving relevance, so it is the wrong move on both counts. D — more reasoning over mostly irrelevant text does not create relevance, and it increases cost further."},

{id:1433,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement best captures the relationship between a model's context window and its performance on long inputs?",
o:["A. Anything that fits inside the window is processed with uniform reliability, which is what the window guarantees","B. The window is a hard capacity limit; reasoning quality over what fits is a separate, empirical property that degrades as irrelevant volume grows","C. Performance improves monotonically with context length, because the model has more information to work with","D. Reasoning quality is determined by `max_tokens`, not by input length"],a:1,
e:"The window answers a capacity question and nothing more. Quality over a filled window is measured, not guaranteed: as token count grows, accuracy and recall degrade. That splits the design work in two. Capacity planning uses `count_tokens` plus headroom for generated output including thinking. Quality assurance needs an eval on representative queries at the context size you intend to ship. Principle: fitting the window and reasoning well over it are different claims requiring different evidence.",
w:"A — the window bounds capacity and makes no claim about quality of reasoning at any fill level. C — beyond the relevant material, extra context adds noise and cost rather than accuracy. D — `max_tokens` caps output length and has no bearing on how well the model reasons over its input."},

{id:1434,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An architect validates a 700,000-token single-request design by running `count_tokens`, confirming the figure fits the model's window, and signing off. What has been established, and what has not?",
o:["A. Both fit and quality, because the endpoint reports the exact figure the model will see","B. Neither, because `count_tokens` returns an estimate and cannot be relied on for capacity planning","C. Capacity is established approximately — the input fits, provided room is also reserved for generated output including thinking — but nothing has been established about answer quality at that size, which requires an eval on representative queries","D. Quality is established, because a payload that fits the window is processed uniformly; only cost remains unknown"],a:2,
e:"`count_tokens` is free, tokenizer-specific and returns an **estimate** of `input_tokens` covering messages, system and tools. It answers \"does the input fit\", and even then only once you subtract a reservation for output and thinking, which share the same window. Whether the model can locate and reason over the right detail across 700,000 tokens is an empirical question, and accuracy is known to degrade as volume grows. Run the eval before committing to a single-giant-request design; retrieval or per-document requests are often cheaper and better.",
w:"A — a token count says nothing about reasoning quality, and it is an estimate rather than an exact figure. B — an estimate is entirely usable for capacity planning with headroom; rejecting it leaves you with character heuristics, which are worse. D — uniform processing across a full window is not a property the API offers, and long-context detail recall demonstrably varies."},

{id:1435,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A due-diligence tool places its task instruction and required output format at the very top of a request, followed by 300,000 tokens of appended documents. Outputs increasingly ignore the required format as the document set grows. Which mitigation is soundest?",
o:["A. Keep the stable instruction block at the front so it remains cacheable, restate the output contract immediately after the documents where it sits closest to generation, and validate the format deterministically after the call","B. Move the instruction into the final user message only, so it is the last thing the model reads","C. Repeat the full instruction block every 20,000 tokens throughout the documents","D. Raise `max_tokens` so the model has room to comply with the format"],a:0,
e:"Instructions competing with hundreds of thousands of tokens of material lose influence, and recency helps — but wholesale relocation is costly, because the cacheable prefix is ordered **tools then system then messages** and must be byte-identical to be reused. The workable shape combines all three layers: stable prefix first for cost, a short restatement of the contract late for adherence, and `output_config.format` with constrained decoding plus post-hoc validation for the actual guarantee. Principle: prompting improves the odds, code delivers the requirement.",
w:"B — that discards a large cacheable stable prefix and interleaves instructions with volatile content, raising cost for a partial adherence gain. C — repeated blocks add tens of thousands of billed tokens, dilute the signal further, and can conflict with one another. D — output capacity is not the constraint; the model is producing output, just not in the required shape."},

{id:1436,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A retailer's assistant must answer \"how many of SKU 4471 are in the Leeds warehouse right now?\" Which mechanism supplies that?",
o:["A. Weight adaptation on historical inventory snapshots, so the model learns the stock patterns","B. A larger context window, so more of the inventory database fits in the prompt","C. A stronger model tier, since the question demands precise numerical reasoning","D. Retrieval — call the inventory system at request time and place the returned figure in context, so the answer is grounded in live data"],a:3,
e:"Three mechanisms answer three different needs. **Parametric knowledge** is what is in the weights, fixed at training time, and cannot contain today's stock level. **Weight adaptation** changes behaviour and bakes in facts that are stale the moment they change. **Retrieval** is the mechanism whose freshness you control, because it reads the system of record on every request. Principle: match the mechanism to the requirement — behaviour versus facts, and if facts, how fast they change and who controls that cadence.",
w:"A — training on snapshots yields knowledge of past patterns rather than the current count, and it is stale immediately. B — a larger window lets you paste more data but does not fetch anything; something still has to read the live system. C — the difficulty is data currency, not arithmetic, and a stronger model with no live figure still has nothing correct to say."},

{id:1437,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which mechanism gives a model access to information that was not in its training data?",
o:["A. Increasing the effort setting","B. Increasing `max_tokens`","C. Putting the information into the request context — retrieval results, tool results, or attached documents","D. Increasing the size of the context window"],a:2,
e:"A model can only reason over two things: what is encoded in its weights, and what is in the request. Anything outside the training data therefore has to arrive as context — retrieved passages, tool call results, attached documents or images. That is why retrieval and tool use are the load-bearing mechanisms for currency and for private data, and why both come with a citable source you can validate. Principle: knowledge you did not put in the request is knowledge the model may not have.",
w:"A — effort changes how much the model reasons, not what it knows. B — `max_tokens` caps output length only. D — a larger window raises the ceiling on how much you can supply, but it does not supply anything."},

{id:1438,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team has two requirements: outputs must follow a consistent house style and section structure, and the assistant must know this week's price list. They hold 50,000 labelled style examples. What is the correct mapping of mechanism to requirement?",
o:["A. Weight adaptation on the 50,000 examples addresses both, since the examples also contain pricing","B. Style is a behavioural requirement — address it with instructions, few-shot examples and native structured output first, and weight adaptation only if that measurably plateaus; the price list is a factual-currency requirement and must come from retrieval at request time","C. Both are prompt problems, and a sufficiently detailed system prompt covers pricing as well as style","D. Both are retrieval problems — retrieve style exemplars and prices together on every request"],a:1,
e:"Separate the axes. Form and behaviour respond to instruction, examples and output constraints, and can be baked into weights once volume justifies the cost. Facts that change on a cadence you do not control must be read at request time, or you simply re-inherit staleness in a slower-moving form. Note the ordering too: prompting and `output_config.format` are cheap, immediate and measurable, so establish that they have genuinely plateaued before reaching for adaptation. Principle: bake behaviour, retrieve facts.",
w:"A — weight adaptation freezes whatever prices the examples contain, which is exactly the staleness being avoided. C — a hand-maintained price list in the system prompt is stale as soon as prices move, and it is paid for on every request. D — retrieving exemplars per request is possible but is an expensive way to enforce a fixed house style, and it does not make the style consistent."},

{id:1439,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What is \"parametric knowledge\", and what is its principal limitation for application design?",
o:["A. The information currently in the request context; it is limited by the context window","B. The retrieved passages the model was given; it is limited by retriever recall","C. A cached copy of previous conversations; it is limited by the cache TTL","D. What is encoded in the model's weights from training; it has a cutoff, cannot be updated per request, and carries no source you can cite or verify"],a:3,
e:"Parametric knowledge is the model's built-in prior. Three limits follow and they drive architecture. It has a **cutoff**, so anything newer is absent. It is **not addressable**, so you cannot update or retract one fact for one request. And it carries **no provenance**, so a claim drawn from it cannot be attributed or checked. Anything requiring currency, tenancy or auditability therefore belongs in context via retrieval or tool calls, where each of those three properties is recoverable.",
w:"A — that describes context, which is supplied per request and freely replaceable, and is precisely what parametric knowledge is not. B — retrieved passages are context too, and they arrive with a citable source. C — prompt caching stores computed prefix state to reduce input cost and is not a knowledge store."},

{id:1440,d:"D2",s:"LLM Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team adapts a model's weights on their full product documentation to stop it giving outdated answers. Six weeks later three new products ship and the assistant is again wrong about them. What does this show?",
o:["A. Weight adaptation freezes facts at adaptation time, so factual currency is not a problem it can solve; anything that changes on its own schedule belongs in retrieval","B. The adaptation used too few examples; a larger corpus would have generalised to the new products","C. The adaptation needs a higher effort setting at inference time to surface the newer material","D. The new products should have been added permanently to the system prompt"],a:0,
e:"Adaptation is a snapshot; retrieval is a pipeline. Beyond staleness, a snapshot gives you no citation for the claims it produces, no way to retract a single fact, and a full cycle of cost and evaluation for every refresh. Where the requirement is \"always current\", the mechanism must read the source of truth at request time. Keep adaptation for behaviour that is genuinely stable — format, tone, task shape — and let facts arrive as context. Principle: bake behaviour, retrieve facts.",
w:"B — no quantity of examples about existing products contains information about products that did not yet exist. C — effort controls reasoning depth, not access to facts that were never encoded. D — a permanently maintained prompt block is a hand-updated stale copy that is also billed on every request, recreating the same problem more expensively."},

{id:1441,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A platform team's Claude gateway currently owns each product's prompt templates, few-shot examples and output schemas, so any prompt change requires a gateway release. Eight product teams are blocked behind a fortnightly deploy train. Which split is correct?",
o:["A. Move prompt content, examples and schemas back to the owning applications; keep the gateway on cross-cutting concerns — key custody, retry and backoff policy, request and token-rate throttling, per-tenant token accounting, structured logging with `request-id`, and redaction before persistence","B. Keep everything in the gateway but add per-team feature flags so prompts can be switched without a deploy","C. Move retry and rate-limit handling into each application so the gateway only forwards requests","D. Split the gateway into eight per-team gateways, each owning its own prompts and its own key"],a:0,
e:"A gateway earns its place by centralising what must be uniform, auditable, and duplicated nowhere: the credential, one retry and backoff policy honouring `retry-after`, concurrency and token-rate shaping across the shared organisation limits, per-tenant accounting from the `usage` block, and redaction before anything is written to logs. Task-specific content is the mirror image — owned by one team, changing at that team's cadence, released with that team's code. Principle: centralise the invariant, distribute the variable.",
w:"B — flags let you toggle prompts but leave prompt content in another team's repository and release cycle, so the ownership problem is unchanged. C — retry and rate-limit policy is exactly what should be uniform, and duplicating it across eight applications guarantees divergent, uncoordinated backoff against one shared limit. D — per-team gateways multiply key custody, throttling and accounting surfaces, discarding the reason for having a gateway."},

{id:1442,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A single-page application calls the Claude API directly from the browser. The key is injected at build time from an environment variable, so it never appears in source control. A security review flags it. What is the accurate assessment and fix?",
o:["A. It is acceptable, because the key was never committed to source control","B. It is acceptable if the key is scoped to a workspace with a low spend limit","C. It is acceptable if the SPA sends the key on `Authorization: Bearer` rather than `x-api-key`","D. Build-time injection places the key in the shipped bundle, where any user can read it — the call must move behind a server-side gateway that holds the key and authorises each request against the user's authenticated session"],a:3,
e:"A build-time environment variable is compile-time substitution: the literal ends up in the JavaScript that is served, readable by anyone who opens developer tools, and unrotatable without a redeploy. Anything a browser can send, an attacker can send. The gateway holds the credential, authenticates the caller, applies per-user throttling and budget, and never returns the key. Principle: credentials live only on servers you control, and authorisation is checked against the **authenticated session**, never against an identity the client asserts.",
w:"A — the exposure is the shipped artefact, not the repository, so not committing the key changes nothing about who can read the bundle. B — a spend cap limits the damage from abuse without preventing it, and does nothing to stop the key being used. C — both credential headers are valid for a server-side call; changing which one the browser sends does not make a browser a safe place for a secret."},

{id:1443,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A mobile app embeds the API key in its binary, XOR-obfuscated and reassembled at runtime. Why is this not an adequate control?",
o:["A. Because obfuscated keys are rejected by the API","B. Because the obfuscation adds latency to every call","C. Because anything shipped to a device can be extracted from it — obfuscation raises effort, not the security boundary, and the key still cannot be rotated without an app release","D. Because mobile clients must use `Authorization: Bearer` and cannot send `x-api-key`"],a:2,
e:"A secret on a device you do not control is a published secret with extra steps: the binary can be unpacked, the runtime memory inspected, and the assembled key lifted. Rotation is worse still, since it now depends on an app store release and on users updating. The correct shape is unchanged by platform — the key lives in a server-side component, the app authenticates to that component, and the server authorises against the session it verified. Principle: obfuscation is a delay, not a boundary.",
w:"A — the API sees an ordinary credential header and cannot tell how the client stored it. B — deobfuscation cost is negligible, and latency is not the objection. D — both credential headers are valid; the problem is where the secret lives, not which header carries it."},

{id:1444,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A gateway must expose token-by-token output to browsers over WebSocket while calling the Messages API with `\"stream\": true`, which returns server-sent events. Which bridging behaviour is correct?",
o:["A. Buffer the whole SSE stream, then send a single WebSocket frame containing the finished message","B. Forward incremental deltas as they arrive, accumulating per content-block `index`, concatenating `partial_json` for `input_json_delta` before parsing, and emitting a terminal frame carrying the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage taken from `message_delta`","C. Relay each SSE frame verbatim and let the browser parse the upstream event types, since the payloads are already JSON","D. Read the final `stop_reason` and usage from `message_stop`, which is the last event in the stream"],a:1,
e:"SSE and WebSocket are two transports for the same logical stream, so the bridge's job is translation plus accumulation. Every block event carries an **`index`**, and deltas for different blocks interleave, so state must be kept per index. `input_json_delta` arrives as `partial_json` fragments that are only valid JSON once concatenated. Crucially, the final `stop_reason` and the cumulative output usage arrive in **`message_delta`**, not `message_stop`. Emit your own downstream protocol so browser clients are not coupled to upstream event names.",
w:"A — buffering discards the only reason to stream, which is time to first token. C — it works until an upstream event name or shape changes, at which point every browser client breaks; decoupling that is the bridge's purpose. D — `message_stop` terminates the stream but is not where the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage are delivered."},

{id:1445,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A WebSocket bridge streams Claude output to a mobile client. On a flaky network the socket drops mid-generation and the client reconnects. Users see the answer restart from nothing, or a blank reply. What does the bridge need?",
o:["A. Ask the API to resume the interrupted generation from where it stopped, using the message id","B. Rely on WebSocket automatic reconnection, which replays missed frames","C. Reduce `max_tokens` so responses finish before the network drops","D. Server-side per-session state — accumulate the response as it streams, keyed by a session id the client presents on reconnect, and replay from the client's cursor — because the API is stateless and there is no upstream stream to resume"],a:3,
e:"The Messages API keeps no server-side session, so an interrupted request cannot be reattached and nothing upstream will replay it. Durability is therefore the bridge's responsibility: allocate a session id, accumulate text as deltas arrive, let a reconnecting client present the id plus a cursor, replay from the cursor, then continue. The same store gives you the terminal `stop_reason` and the `usage` figures for accounting, which would otherwise be lost with the dropped socket.",
w:"A — statelessness means there is no resumable server-side generation to reattach to; a new call is simply new work. B — reconnection re-establishes a socket, but frames sent while it was down are gone and nothing in the protocol buffers them for you. C — shorter responses reduce exposure without solving anything, and they degrade the product to work around a transport problem."},

{id:1446,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A gateway logs full request and response bodies to a central log store so teams can debug prompts. Compliance objects, because requests contain customer names, addresses and claim details. Which design satisfies both needs?",
o:["A. Redact or tokenise identified sensitive fields at the gateway before anything is persisted, and retain non-sensitive diagnostics — `request-id`, model, latency, `stop_reason` and the `usage` token counts — with full bodies available only through a short-retention, access-controlled path where genuinely justified","B. Keep logging full bodies but restrict the log store to the platform team","C. Stop logging entirely and rely on the API's own request history for debugging","D. Hash the request and response bodies so they can be compared without being readable"],a:0,
e:"Redaction must happen at the boundary, **before** persistence, because once sensitive content enters a log pipeline it is replicated, indexed and backed up beyond your reach. Most of what debugging needs is not sensitive anyway: the `request-id` for escalation with support, the model, the `stop_reason`, latency, and the `usage` token counts that also feed cost attribution. Principle: log metadata always, payloads deliberately, and redact before the write rather than after.",
w:"B — narrowing readership does not change the fact that regulated data has been copied into a general-purpose log pipeline that is replicated and retained. C — there is no client-accessible upstream store of your request bodies to fall back on, so you would lose diagnostics altogether. D — a hash is unreadable to your own engineers too, supporting equality checks and nothing that debugging a prompt requires."},

{id:1447,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which set of responsibilities belongs in a shared Claude gateway rather than in each calling application?",
o:["A. Prompt templates, few-shot examples and output schemas for each product","B. API key custody, retry and backoff policy, request and token-rate throttling, per-tenant usage accounting, structured logging, and redaction before persistence","C. Business rules deciding whether a model-proposed action is permitted","D. Model choice and effort settings for every product feature"],a:1,
e:"The gateway's remit is the cross-cutting, must-be-uniform concerns: one place holding the credential, one retry policy honouring `retry-after`, one throttle shaping traffic against the shared organisation rate limits, one accounting path reading the `usage` block per tenant, and redaction before logs are written. Everything task-specific stays with its owning team, and authorisation stays with the service that owns the resource and the authenticated session. Principle: a gateway is infrastructure, not a place to put other teams' business logic.",
w:"A — task-specific content is owned by one team and changes at that team's cadence, so centralising it couples eight release cycles together. C — authorisation must run in the service that owns the resource and the authenticated session, not in a shared proxy that has neither. D — a gateway can supply defaults and guardrails, but the quality and latency trade-off for a feature belongs to the team that owns the feature."},

{id:1448,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A gateway catches upstream failures and returns its own `{\"code\": \"UPSTREAM_ERROR\"}` to callers, logging only that string. When a persistent 400 has to be escalated, support can correlate nothing. What is the minimum fix?",
o:["A. Return the raw upstream response body to callers unchanged","B. Add a retry so callers see a success more often","C. Capture and propagate the upstream `request-id` header — and the `request_id` in the error body — alongside the upstream `error.type` and message, log them, and surface the identifier to the caller","D. Log the full request body on every failure so it can be replayed"],a:2,
e:"Every response carries a **`request-id`** header, and error bodies carry a top-level **`request_id`**; that identifier is what support needs to trace a specific call. Error bodies also carry a machine-readable `error.type` — `invalid_request_error`, `rate_limit_error`, `overloaded_error` and so on — which is what your retry policy should branch on. Collapsing all of it into one opaque code destroys both diagnosability and correct retry classification. Principle: translate errors at the boundary, but never lose the correlation identifier or the category.",
w:"A — passing the raw body through leaks internal detail to callers and still gives them no stable contract; you want the identifier and category, not the whole body. B — a 400 is a deterministic validation failure, so retrying it fails repeatedly and delays diagnosis. D — full-body logging on every failure is a compliance liability and still does not yield the identifier support needs to trace the call upstream."},

{id:1449,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A gateway wraps every upstream call in a uniform \"retry three times with backoff on any non-2xx\" policy. Latency on malformed requests is now several seconds, and one team's misconfigured schema generated a large volume of pointless traffic. What is wrong?",
o:["A. Three retries is too few; the policy needs more attempts with longer waits","B. Retry policy must branch on error category: retry 429 and server-side failures such as 500 `api_error` and 529 `overloaded_error`, honouring `retry-after` with jitter; never retry deterministic client errors such as 400 `invalid_request_error`, 401, 403 or 413","C. Backoff should be removed so the retries complete faster","D. All retrying should move into the calling applications so each can choose its own policy"],a:1,
e:"The error taxonomy exists so clients can classify. A **400 `invalid_request_error`** fails identically every time, so retrying it multiplies load and adds latency for nothing; `401 authentication_error`, `403 permission_error` and `413 request_too_large` behave the same way. Retryable failures are `429 rate_limit_error` and server-side conditions such as `500` and `529`, and on a 429 the `retry-after` header states how long to wait. Add jitter, or a fleet retries in lockstep and rebuilds the wave it was recovering from.",
w:"A — more attempts against a deterministic validation failure waste more capacity and add more latency. C — removing backoff turns transient throttling into a tight retry storm, which is how a 429 wave becomes self-sustaining. D — per-application policies are exactly what the gateway exists to prevent, since uncoordinated backoff across teams contends for one shared rate limit."},

{id:1450,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A gateway attributes cost per team by summing `usage.input_tokens + usage.output_tokens` per request and multiplying by a blended rate. Finance finds the totals diverge materially from the invoice, and the divergence is concentrated in the teams that use prompt caching most heavily. What is missing?",
o:["A. Nothing in the accounting; the divergence must come from requests that bypassed the gateway","B. Output tokens should be excluded, since they are already reflected in the next turn's input count","C. `usage` figures are estimates, so per-team attribution can only ever be approximate","D. Cached tokens are reported in separate fields and priced differently — account for `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` separately from ordinary `input_tokens`, since a write costs more than base input and a read costs a small fraction of it"],a:3,
e:"`usage` separates ordinary input, cache writes and cache reads because they carry different rates: a write is charged above base input, higher again for the longer TTL, and a read at a small fraction of it. Summing them as one number over-charges read-heavy teams and under-charges write-heavy ones. Keep the fields distinct per tenant, price each with its own multiplier, and alert on the write-to-read ratio, since a high write share means the cache is being paid for and not reused.",
w:"A — a systematic divergence concentrated in the caching-heavy teams points at how cached tokens are priced, not at traffic taking another path. B — output tokens are billed as output when generated and again as input when resent in history, so excluding them under-counts twice over. C — `usage` reports the actual billed token counts for the call; it is `count_tokens` that returns a pre-flight estimate."},

{id:1451,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A gateway starts receiving 400 `invalid_request_error` responses on requests that were succeeding an hour earlier, with no code change. Its generic policy retries them and requests pile up. What is the likely cause and the correct handling?",
o:["A. It is a transient upstream condition; keep retrying with longer backoff until it clears","B. The monthly usage-tier cap has been reached, which is always reported as a 400","C. A configured organisation or workspace spend limit has been hit — a 400 is a deterministic configuration state, so the gateway must fail fast, surface it to operators as a budget condition and stop retrying; a usage-tier monthly cap surfaces instead as a 429","D. The API key has been revoked, which surfaces as a 400"],a:2,
e:"The two budget conditions surface differently, and the difference dictates behaviour. A **configured** organisation or workspace spend limit returns **400** — a deterministic state no retry will clear, so it must page an operator to raise or reallocate the limit. A **usage-tier monthly cap** returns **429**, alongside ordinary rate limiting. The practical consequence is that neither should be your first warning: burn-rate alerting from your own token accounting should fire well before the API begins rejecting traffic.",
w:"A — a 400 is a deterministic client-side condition, and no amount of backoff makes a configured spend limit clear itself. B — a usage-tier monthly cap surfaces as a 429, which is exactly why the two must be distinguished. D — a revoked or invalid credential returns 401 `authentication_error`, not a 400."},

{id:1452,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A finance partner asks to be warned before Claude spend overruns, not after. The gateway currently records nothing but request counts. What is the minimum that delivers useful early warning?",
o:["A. Persist per-tenant `usage` token counts per request together with the model used, price each token class at its own rate, aggregate into a running spend figure, and alert on burn rate against the period budget rather than on a crossed total","B. Poll for the month-to-date invoice figure hourly and alert when it crosses a threshold","C. Alert whenever the 429 rate rises, since throttling correlates with heavy spend","D. Set the organisation spend limit slightly below budget so the API stops traffic at the ceiling"],a:0,
e:"Early warning needs a leading indicator you compute yourself, and every response already gives you one: `usage` reports consumption per token class, so per-request accounting plus rates yields spend in near real time. Alert on **rate of burn** and projection to period end rather than on a crossed total, and break it down per tenant, model and route so you know whose traffic moved. Keep the configured spend limit as a hard backstop; it is a circuit breaker, not a monitor — it returns a deterministic 400 and stops traffic.",
w:"B — a periodic total is a lagging, coarse indicator and says nothing about which tenant or route caused the move. C — throttling reflects rate-limit pressure rather than cost: a cheap high-frequency route can throttle while spend is flat, and an expensive low-volume route can blow the budget without a single 429. D — a hard limit produces a deterministic rejection and stops traffic, which is an outage rather than a warning."},

{id:1453,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A batch enrichment job sends requests of roughly 30,000 input tokens and 200 output tokens each. At 40 workers it sustains a steady rate; at 200 workers throughput drops, the 429 rate climbs and `retry-after` values grow. What is the correct diagnosis?",
o:["A. The output-token-per-minute limit is binding; reduce `max_tokens` to relieve it","B. The requests-per-minute limit is binding; consolidate several documents into each request","C. The input-token-per-minute limit is binding, because the workload is input-heavy — concurrency cannot raise a token-rate ceiling, so the fix is a bounded worker pool sized to the token budget plus jittered backoff honouring `retry-after`","D. The gateway's connection pool is exhausted; raise the pool size to match the worker count"],a:2,
e:"Rate limits are enforced on three dimensions — requests per minute, **input** tokens per minute and **output** tokens per minute — and which one binds depends on traffic shape. At 30,000 input against 200 output tokens, this job saturates the input-token ceiling long before the request ceiling. Adding workers cannot raise a ceiling; it converts the excess into 429s, retries and wasted capacity. Size concurrency to the token budget, queue the remainder, and back off with jitter on the advertised delay. Latency-tolerant bulk work of this shape also belongs on the Batch API.",
w:"A — the workload generates almost no output, so the output-token ceiling is not binding, and `max_tokens` does not factor into output-token rate limits in any case. B — consolidating documents lowers request count while sending the same or more input tokens per minute, so it does not relieve the saturated dimension. D — a local pool limit would appear as client-side queueing or connection errors, not as upstream 429s with growing `retry-after` values."},

{id:1454,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A gateway must shape traffic so that a shared organisation rate limit is not exhausted. What should it meter?",
o:["A. All three dimensions the limits are enforced on — requests per minute, input tokens per minute and output tokens per minute — since which one binds depends on the shape of each workload","B. Requests per minute only, since token counts are not knowable before the call","C. Output tokens only, since output is the expensive side","D. `max_tokens`, since it determines the output rate a request will consume"],a:0,
e:"Rate limiting operates on three dimensions, and a shaper that meters fewer than three leaves a ceiling unguarded. Read the `anthropic-ratelimit-*` headers to track remaining headroom per dimension, estimate input with `count_tokens` before dispatch, and accumulate actual consumption from the `usage` block afterwards. Honour `retry-after` on a 429 rather than guessing. Principle: throttle on the quantity the limit is expressed in, not on a proxy that happens to be easier to count.",
w:"B — request counting alone lets a handful of very large prompts saturate the input-token ceiling while the request meter reads nearly idle, and input size is knowable in advance via `count_tokens`. C — an input-heavy workload can exhaust the input-token ceiling with negligible output, so metering one side leaves the other unguarded. D — `max_tokens` is a cap the model need not reach and does not factor into output-token rate limits; metering reported usage reflects real consumption."},

{id:1455,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A shared gateway queues all inbound work in a single global FIFO. One team submits a 50,000-request backfill and interactive chat traffic from every other team goes from 2-second to 90-second responses. What is the structural fix?",
o:["A. Increase the global concurrency ceiling so the queue drains faster","B. Separate the workloads — per-tenant queues with fair scheduling and per-tenant concurrency caps, plus a distinct low-priority lane for bulk work so interactive traffic is never queued behind a backfill","C. Give the backfill a longer client timeout so its requests stop being retried","D. Remove the queue and let requests hit the upstream API directly, so its own rate limiting arbitrates"],a:1,
e:"A single FIFO makes latency a function of who queued first, so one bulk submitter monopolises a shared ceiling — classic head-of-line blocking. The queue is where fairness has to be expressed: isolate tenants, cap each tenant's in-flight share, and class traffic so bulk work consumes only the capacity interactive work is not using. Bulk latency-tolerant work also has a cheaper home in the **Batch API**, which trades a completion window for a substantial discount and removes that load from the interactive path entirely.",
w:"A — raising the ceiling increases pressure on the shared upstream rate limits and still serves the backfill first, leaving the ordering problem untouched. C — timeouts change how long a caller waits, not who is served next. D — removing the queue converts head-of-line blocking into a 429 storm shared by every team, with no fairness at all."},

{id:1456,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A gateway's HTTP timeout is 30 seconds. A long generation exceeds it, the client library retries, and a downstream workflow triggered by the response runs twice — two tickets are created for one user request. What must the gateway add?",
o:["A. A longer timeout, so retries stop happening","B. A deduplication check on the response text, discarding a second identical response","C. Reliance on the API to recognise and collapse the duplicate request","D. Idempotency at the gateway — accept a client-supplied idempotency key, record the outcome against it, and return the stored result for a repeat of the same key rather than issuing a second upstream call"],a:3,
e:"Retries are a fact of distributed systems, so at-least-once delivery has to be made **safe** rather than prevented. The gateway is the right place: key each logical operation, persist the first outcome, and serve replays from that record. Note that the Messages API is **stateless** and holds no notion of your request's identity, so a repeat is simply new work with new cost. Principle: make the effect idempotent at the boundary that owns the side effect, and keep side effects out of the retry path.",
w:"A — a longer timeout narrows the window without removing it, and any network-level retry reopens it. B — generation is probabilistic, so two responses to the same prompt often differ textually while representing the same duplicated operation. C — the API keeps no per-request state to deduplicate against; an identical repeat is processed as a second request."},

{id:1457,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Why can a client not rely on the Messages API to collapse a retried, byte-identical request into one?",
o:["A. Because the API is stateless — it holds no server-side record of your prior requests to match against, so an identical repeat is processed as new work","B. Because deduplication applies only to requests that also carry `cache_control`","C. Because deduplication is enabled only for the Batch API","D. Because byte-identical requests are rare, so the optimisation is not worth applying"],a:0,
e:"Statelessness is the reason, and it is architectural rather than a policy choice: there is no session, no stored request history and no request identity for the service to compare against. Everything the model should see must be resent, and everything resent is new billable work. The consequence for design is direct — deduplication, idempotency keys, conversation state and audit records are all your responsibility, and the natural place for them is the gateway that owns the side effects.",
w:"B — `cache_control` reuses computation over an input prefix to reduce input cost; it neither suppresses a duplicate call nor returns a stored response. C — batch requests carry a `custom_id` so results can be matched to inputs, which is not deduplication of repeated submissions. D — the reason is the absence of any state in which a prior request could be remembered, not a judgement about frequency."},

{id:1458,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A synchronous REST endpoint wraps Claude for long report generation. p50 latency is 12 seconds and p99 is 120 seconds. It sits behind a load balancer with a 30-second idle timeout. Users requesting long reports see a 504 while gateway logs show the upstream call completing normally. What are the correct options?",
o:["A. Raise the load balancer idle timeout to 180 seconds and leave the request shape unchanged","B. Reduce `max_tokens` until p99 falls below 30 seconds","C. Either stream the response so bytes flow continuously and the connection never idles, or convert the endpoint to an asynchronous shape that returns a job identifier immediately and delivers the result by polling or callback","D. Add a retry on 504 so the client transparently re-requests"],a:2,
e:"A 504 with a healthy upstream is an idle-timeout symptom: a non-streaming call sends nothing until the whole message is ready, so any intermediary with a shorter idle budget cuts the connection. Two shapes fix it properly. **Streaming** keeps the socket active and delivers time to first token as a bonus — it is also why SDKs require streaming for very large `max_tokens`. An **asynchronous** shape decouples client wait from generation time entirely, which is right whenever work can outlive a reasonable HTTP request.",
w:"A — pushing every timeout in the chain up to the worst case keeps a connection blocked for minutes and merely moves the ceiling, which the next slower report will reach. B — truncating reports to fit a network timeout degrades the product to work around a transport limit, and hitting the cap returns `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` with a cut-off report. D — retrying a request that already completed upstream doubles the cost and duplicates any side effects, without making the second attempt faster."},

{id:1459,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Why do the official SDKs require streaming for requests with very large `max_tokens`?",
o:["A. Because non-streaming responses are capped at a smaller output size","B. Because streaming reduces the number of output tokens billed","C. Because `stop_reason` is only reported on streaming requests","D. Because a single non-streaming response can take long enough to exceed HTTP timeouts, whereas a stream delivers bytes continuously and keeps the connection active"],a:3,
e:"This is a transport constraint, not a model one. A non-streaming request holds an idle connection for the entire generation, so a very large output cap can exceed client, proxy or load-balancer timeouts before a single byte is returned. Streaming keeps the connection active and additionally gives you time to first token and the ability to render progressively. Remember where the terminal information lives: the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage arrive in `message_delta`, not `message_stop`.",
w:"A — the output ceiling is a property of the model, not of the transport. B — the same tokens are generated and billed either way, since streaming changes delivery rather than consumption. C — `stop_reason` is present on both shapes; on a stream its final value arrives in `message_delta`."},

{id:1460,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team converts a slow Claude-backed endpoint to an asynchronous shape. What must the contract include for it to be usable?",
o:["A. A blocking long-poll that holds the connection until the job completes, so clients need to keep no state","B. Immediate acknowledgement with a job identifier, a status resource distinguishing pending from terminal states, a way to fetch the result and any error detail including the upstream `request-id`, and a documented result retention window","C. Only the job identifier, since clients can retry the original request until it succeeds","D. A webhook callback carrying the result and nothing else, since polling becomes redundant once callbacks exist"],a:1,
e:"An asynchronous shape moves waiting out of the HTTP request, and the burden lands on the contract. Clients must distinguish \"not finished\" from \"finished and failed\", retrieve the outcome, receive the correlation identifier needed to escalate with support, and know how long the result remains available. Callbacks and polling are complements, not alternatives: deliveries can be lost, so the status resource is the reconciliation path. Genuinely bulk, latency-tolerant work should go to the Batch API instead, which completes within its own window at a substantial discount.",
w:"A — a long-poll reintroduces precisely the held connection and intermediary idle timeout that the asynchronous shape was adopted to escape. C — re-issuing the original request is not a status check; it starts new billable work and duplicates side effects. D — a callback-only contract has no recovery path when a delivery is lost, and no way for a client to ask about a job it has heard nothing about."},

{id:1461,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"An SDK client is configured with a 10-minute request timeout and the SDK's default retry behaviour. What is the worst-case wall-clock time a single call can occupy?",
o:["A. 10 minutes, because the timeout bounds the whole operation including retries","B. 10 minutes plus the backoff waits, because retries share the original deadline","C. Unbounded, because timed-out requests are retried indefinitely","D. Roughly the timeout multiplied by the number of attempts, plus backoff, because the timeout applies per attempt and a timeout is itself a retryable failure"],a:3,
e:"SDK timeouts are **per attempt**, and a timed-out attempt counts as retryable, so the effective ceiling is attempts multiplied by the timeout plus the backoff waits. With a generous timeout and the default retry count, that is far longer than most callers expect and it interacts badly with an outer HTTP deadline. Set an overall budget in your own gateway and derive the per-attempt timeout and attempt count from it. Note also that the SDKs' timeout **units differ by language** — seconds in some, milliseconds in others.",
w:"A — the configured value bounds a single attempt, not the sequence of attempts. B — retries do not inherit a shared deadline; each new attempt starts its own timeout. C — retries are bounded by the configured maximum, so the worst case is large but finite."},

{id:1462,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A gateway enforces a concurrency ceiling with a bounded worker pool and an inbound queue. Which signal indicates the ceiling is set too low rather than too high?",
o:["A. A rising upstream 429 rate with growing `retry-after` values","B. Rising time-in-queue and queue depth while the upstream 429 rate stays near zero and rate-limit headroom remains","C. Rising p99 latency on the upstream call itself","D. A rising share of responses carrying `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`"],a:1,
e:"A concurrency ceiling is correctly sized when it saturates the available token-rate budget without provoking throttling, so diagnose from both sides. Pressure inside your queue with no pressure upstream means you are the bottleneck and can raise the ceiling. Upstream 429s with a shallow queue mean the ceiling exceeds what the rate limits allow and should come down. Publish queue depth, queue wait, in-flight count, the 429 rate and the `anthropic-ratelimit-*` headroom together, or the two cases are indistinguishable.",
w:"A — 429s with lengthening advertised delays are the signature of a ceiling set too high for the available token rate, which is the opposite condition. C — upstream latency reflects generation time and prompt size and moves for reasons unrelated to how many calls you permit in parallel. D — that stop reason means responses are hitting your configured output cap, which is a `max_tokens` matter rather than a concurrency one."},

{id:1463,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which languages have official Anthropic SDKs?",
o:["A. Python, TypeScript, C#, Go, Java, PHP and Ruby — seven in total","B. Python and TypeScript only; every other language must use raw HTTP","C. Python, TypeScript, Go, Rust and Swift","D. Python, TypeScript, Java and Kotlin, with Kotlin shipped as a separate package"],a:0,
e:"There are **seven** official SDKs: Python, TypeScript, C#, Go, Java, PHP and Ruby. Kotlin and Scala are served by the Java SDK, and JavaScript by the TypeScript one. What they absorb is the unglamorous correctness: credential and version headers, retry classification with backoff, incremental stream accumulation keyed by content-block `index`, typed error classes, and per-attempt timeouts. Where no SDK exists the REST API is a plain HTTP interface, but you then own all of that yourself.",
w:"B — the official set is far wider than two languages, and choosing raw HTTP where an SDK exists means reimplementing retries, streaming accumulation and typed errors for no benefit. C — Rust and Swift have no official SDK; those projects use raw HTTP or community libraries. D — Kotlin and Scala are covered by the Java SDK rather than separate packages, and C#, Go, PHP and Ruby are missing from that list."},

{id:1464,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team's service is written in Rust, for which there is no official SDK. What is the sound approach?",
o:["A. Add a Python sidecar purely to hold the Anthropic SDK, and call it over local HTTP from Rust","B. Rewrite the service in one of the supported languages","C. Call the REST API over HTTP directly, and budget for reimplementing what an SDK would have provided — credential and `anthropic-version` headers, SSE parsing with per-`index` accumulation, retry classification with backoff and `retry-after`, typed errors, and timeout handling","D. Use an OpenAI-compatible shim so an existing Rust client library can be reused"],a:2,
e:"Raw HTTP is a legitimate path: the API is an ordinary REST interface over `POST /v1/messages`, and the seven official SDKs are conveniences rather than requirements. The trade-off should be explicit, because you take on version pinning, streaming accumulation (`index`-keyed blocks, `partial_json` concatenation, final `stop_reason` and usage from `message_delta`), classification of retryable against deterministic errors, jittered backoff, and timeout budgeting. Write it once behind a thin internal client, not at every call site.",
w:"A — a sidecar buys SDK behaviour at the price of an extra process, hop and failure mode; consider it only where the reimplementation cost genuinely dominates. B — rewriting a service to obtain a client library is disproportionate when the API is ordinary REST over HTTPS. D — a compatibility shim hides the real request and response shapes, so features and error semantics silently fail to map."},

{id:1465,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A Python team hand-rolls HTTP calls to avoid a dependency. Over two months they fix: streamed JSON parsed before a delta was complete, retries firing on 400s, absent jitter causing synchronised retry waves, and errors matched by substring. What is the accurate lesson?",
o:["A. Their mistake was choosing Python; a statically typed language would have prevented these defects","B. Each defect corresponds to something the official SDK already implements — incremental stream accumulation, retry classification with backoff, and typed exception classes — so raw HTTP is a deliberate choice for when there is no SDK or a hard constraint, not a default","C. Raw HTTP is always wrong, and the API should only ever be called through an SDK","D. The defects arose because the API is under-specified, and the SDK merely papers over that"],a:1,
e:"An SDK's value is accumulated correctness in the unexciting parts: `index`-keyed content-block accumulation, concatenating `partial_json` before parsing, retrying 429 and 5xx while leaving 400 alone, honouring `retry-after` with jitter, per-attempt timeouts, and exception types you can branch on instead of substring matching. Reimplementing that is a real project with a real defect budget, as this team discovered. Choose raw HTTP deliberately — unsupported language, embedded runtime, hard dependency policy — then encapsulate it once.",
w:"A — every one of these defects is language-independent, and a type checker does not tell you which status codes are retryable. C — raw HTTP is explicitly supported and is the right answer for unsupported languages and constrained environments. D — the streaming events, error taxonomy and rate-limit headers are all documented; the defects came from not implementing them."},

{id:1466,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which of these does an official SDK provide that a raw HTTP integration must implement itself?",
o:["A. Retry classification with backoff on retryable statuses, incremental stream accumulation, typed error classes, and per-request timeout handling","B. A server-side conversation store, so history need not be resent","C. Automatic deduplication of identical requests","D. Automatic reduction of input tokens to fit the context window"],a:0,
e:"An SDK is a client-side convenience layer over the same REST API, so what it adds is client-side machinery: classifying which failures are retryable and backing off with jitter, accumulating streamed content blocks by `index`, exposing typed exceptions instead of raw status codes, and applying per-attempt timeouts. What no SDK adds is server behaviour. Statelessness, the absence of deduplication and the absence of silent truncation are properties of the API, identical in every language and in raw HTTP.",
w:"B — the API is stateless in every client; no SDK holds your conversation, and the full history is resent on every call. C — nothing deduplicates requests at either layer, so idempotency is yours to implement. D — no client silently truncates input; an oversized request fails explicitly rather than being trimmed for you."},

{id:1467,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A conversational gateway keeps each conversation's message history in a process-local dictionary. It works on a single instance. After a second instance is added behind a round-robin load balancer, roughly half of all follow-up turns lose context. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Enable sticky sessions on the load balancer so each conversation always reaches the same instance","B. Have the API retain the conversation server-side by sending only the latest turn plus the previous message id","C. Externalise conversation state to a shared store the gateway reads and writes per turn, because the Messages API is stateless and the full accumulated history must be resent on every call","D. Broadcast every turn to all instances so each holds a complete copy of every conversation"],a:2,
e:"Because the API keeps no session, the conversation lives wherever your architecture puts it — and process-local memory is not a place a horizontally scaled service can put it. Move it to a shared store keyed by conversation: load the history for the turn, append, persist. That also creates the seams statelessness demands anyway — trimming or summarising history to bound growth and cost, and a durable record for audit. Principle: statelessness does not remove state, it relocates responsibility for it to you.",
w:"A — sticky sessions hide the defect behind routing, and the state is still lost on deploy, crash, rebalance or scale-in. B — there is no server-side continuation to reference; the API has no notion of a prior message to resume from. D — replicating all state to all instances is a distributed cache with no invalidation story, and it does not survive a restart either."},

{id:1468,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What follows, for cost, from the Messages API being stateless?",
o:["A. Nothing — earlier turns are billed once, on the turn in which they first appear","B. Cost per turn is constant, because only the newest message is charged","C. Cost falls as a conversation lengthens, because more of the prefix becomes cacheable at no charge","D. Input cost per turn grows with conversation length, because the whole accumulated history is resent and re-billed on every call — which is why bounding history and caching stable prefixes are architectural concerns rather than micro-optimisations"],a:3,
e:"Statelessness means every call carries the full transcript, so input cost is a function of conversation length and grows monotonically unless something intervenes. Two levers follow directly, and both belong in the design rather than in a later tuning pass. **Bound the history**: clear stale tool results, summarise completed work, keep durable state in your own store. **Cache the stable prefix** in tools-then-system-then-messages order, where a read costs a fraction of base input — noting that cached tokens still occupy the context window.",
w:"A — a stateless API stores nothing to refer back to, so every earlier turn is transmitted and charged again on every subsequent call. B — the request grows with each turn, so its input cost grows with it. C — cache reads are charged at a small fraction of base input rather than nothing, and only a byte-identical prefix qualifies, so caching slows the growth without reversing it."},

{id:1469,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A multi-turn document review tool attaches the same four scanned pages as `base64` images and re-sends all of them on every turn of a twelve-turn conversation. Request sizes are large and latency is poor. What is the appropriate change?",
o:["A. Downscale the images below the long-edge limit so fewer visual tokens are consumed","B. Move the images into the system prompt so they are sent once","C. Upload each image once via the Files API and reference it by `file_id` on subsequent turns, so the bytes are not retransmitted every turn — noting that on Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud only `base64` image sources are available","D. Ask the model to summarise the images on turn one and drop them from later turns"],a:2,
e:"Statelessness means everything the model should see must be present in every request, which for `base64` images means shipping the bytes twelve times — a bandwidth and latency problem distinct from token cost. Three source types exist: `base64`, `url` and `file`, and the **`file`** type via the Files API exists precisely for multi-turn reuse: upload once, then reference the `file_id`. Token cost is unchanged, since it is counted in visual tokens over 28x28 patches regardless of how the bytes arrive.",
w:"A — downscaling lowers visual tokens, but the resized bytes are still retransmitted on all twelve turns, so the transport problem remains. B — the system prompt is resent on every request like everything else, and bulk content does not belong there. D — a summary discards detail a reviewer may need on a later turn, changing product behaviour to work around a transport issue."},

{id:1470,d:"D2",s:"Technical Fundamentals",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"In a production Claude integration, where must the API key live?",
o:["A. In the client application, injected at build time from an environment variable","B. In the client application, encrypted with a key fetched from the server at startup","C. Anywhere, provided the workspace has a spend limit configured","D. Only in server-side components you control, read from a secret manager or the environment at runtime, never returned to a client, with the client authenticated and authorised by your own service"],a:3,
e:"Key custody is the one gateway responsibility with no acceptable alternative: the credential lives only where you control the runtime, is loaded from a secret manager or environment at start-up, is rotatable without shipping anything to a device, and is never returned in a response. The client authenticates to your service, and your service authorises the request against the **authenticated session** rather than any identity the caller asserts. Both `Authorization: Bearer` and the legacy `x-api-key` header are valid ways to present it — from a server.",
w:"A — build-time injection places the literal in the shipped bundle, where any user can read it. B — a client able to decrypt the key can also leak the decryption path, and shipping both halves to the same device creates no boundary. C — a spend limit caps the blast radius of a leaked key without preventing its misuse, and it surfaces as a hard rejection rather than as protection."}
);
